# Package list for Omarchy quattro on any OSTree/atomic Fedora target -
# secureblue and plain atomic Fedora (Silverblue/Kinoite/Sericea, e.g.
# "Fedora Atomic Sway") alike. The .secureblue filename predates this list
# being shared across both; nothing in it is secureblue-specific (see
# install/helpers/packages.sh, gated on is_ostree(), not is_secureblue()).
# This user's own ~/dotfiles/Brewfile documents the same thing directly:
# "Same list on Fedora Sway Atomic and secureblue."
#
# Format: one entry per line, `<tier>:<name>` or bare `<name>` (defaults to
# system tier). Tiers:
#   system  - rpm-ostree layer. Default when no prefix is given. Right for
#             anything that can't be sandboxed: compositor, portals,
#             PAM/greeter/systemd-service-backed daemons, kernel modules,
#             fonts (needed before any session/sandbox exists).
#   gui:<flatpak-app-id>  - flatpak --user install. <name> is the Flatpak
#             application ID, not the RPM name (they usually differ).
#   cli:<brew-formula>    - brew install. <name> is the linuxbrew formula
#             name (usually, not always, the same as the RPM name).
#
# This is a straight per-package retier of omarchy-base.packages.fedora, not
# an independent list - see that file for the fuller upstream commentary on
# why each package is here at all. Only the packages with an unambiguous,
# well-known Flatpak ID or brew formula were moved off the system tier;
# everything else defaults to system (rpm-ostree) for v1 - correct, if not
# maximally optimal. Revisit case-by-case later; nothing here is final.
#
# The Hyprland stack itself, portals, and the COPR it comes from are handled
# the same as Fedora (see install/helpers/fedora-copr.sh's is_ostree
# branch) - listed here as system tier like everything else that resolves
# from a normal repo, dnf-copr-enabled or not.

# Desktop - Hyprland stack (lionheartp COPR)
hyprland-qt-support
hyprland-guiutils
hyprsunset
hyprpicker
xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
uwsm

# Desktop - Quickshell shell
quickshell

# Desktop - capture
gpu-screen-recorder
wf-recorder
grim
slurp

# Desktop - misc
wl-clipboard
wtype
xdg-user-dirs
qt5-qtwayland
qt6-qtwayland
mesa-vulkan-drivers
yaru-icon-theme
yaru-gtk3-theme
yaru-gtk4-theme
yaru-gtksourceview-theme

# GUI apps - flatpak (clear, well-known app IDs). Nautilus itself NOT
# listed: it's already natively layered on this image's base
# (LayeredPackages already includes `nautilus`) - a flatpak copy on top
# would be a real duplicate (two file managers fighting over the same
# D-Bus/desktop-file identity), which is very likely why installing it as
# a flatpak failed outright on the first real run of this installer.
gui:org.gnome.NautilusPreviewer
gui:org.gnome.Evince
gui:org.kde.kdenlive
gui:com.obsproject.Studio
gui:com.github.PintaProject.Pinta
gui:com.github.xournalpp.xournalpp
gui:org.libreoffice.LibreOffice
gui:org.gnome.Calculator
# GNOME Disks is not published on Flathub - it needs udisks on the host
# anyway, so it stays system tier.
gnome-disk-utility
# Chromium's flatpak sandboxing complicates the native-messaging-host
# registration install/user/chromium.sh does for bundled extensions -
# revisit that script before relying on this; kept system-tier for now so
# the rest of the desktop isn't blocked on that rework.
chromium

# Terminals - kept system-tier: deep Wayland/PTY integration, not a typical
# sandboxed-GUI-app or standalone-CLI-tool shape.
xdg-terminal-exec
alacritty
foot

# Shell & CLI tools - brew (clear, well-known formula names).
#
# packaging/base.sh runs `brew bundle` against ~/dotfiles/Brewfile first
# when that file exists; anything it already installed is skipped here
# ("already installed"), so listing these is harmless on a machine with
# that Brewfile and required on one without it (otherwise nvim, starship
# etc. are simply never installed and lazyvim.sh fails).
cli:starship
cli:eza
cli:fzf
cli:ripgrep
cli:bat
cli:fd
cli:zoxide
cli:tldr
cli:neovim
cli:yt-dlp
cli:btop
# fastfetch and tmux NOT listed here: both are already natively layered on
# this image's base (LayeredPackages already includes both) - listing them
# as cli: too would install a second, brew-sourced copy on top of the
# already-present native one. Confirmed this actually happened on the
# first real run before this fix: `brew list --formula` showed both
# alongside their native package - if you're reading this after that run,
# `brew uninstall fastfetch tmux` cleans up the redundant brew copies.
cli:gum
cli:dua-cli
cli:tmux
# omarchy-menu-keybindings resolves XKB keycodes with xkbcli
# (libxkbcommon-utils) and decodes the Hyprland bind list with Perl's
# JSON::PP - small system libs, not worth a brew detour.
libxkbcommon-utils
perl-JSON-PP
less
man-db
plocate
socat
inotify-tools
inxi
whois
unzip
util-linux-script

# Development - brew where it's a pure userspace CLI.
cli:gh
cli:tree-sitter-cli
cli:ruby
git
cargo
rust
clang
llvm
luarocks
compat-lua
compat-lua-libs
libyaml
python3-gobject
python3-poetry-core
libpq
mariadb-connector-c
java-latest-openjdk
dotnet-runtime-9.0

# Containers - Docker deliberately NOT installed here: Fedora Atomic
# Desktop images (secureblue and plain atomic Fedora alike) ship podman
# instead. See install/config/docker.sh, enable-services.sh, firewall.sh,
# and etc-files.sh, which all skip their Docker-specific steps on any
# OSTree/atomic target for the same reason.
#
# Distrobox, not the toolbox the base image ships: it runs any distro's
# userspace, not just Fedora's, and exports apps and binaries to the host.
# base.sh removes toolbox from the base image once this is layered.
distrobox

# Media & Apps
imv
cli:chafa
mpv
mpv-mpris
ffmpegthumbnailer
ImageMagick
tesseract
tesseract-langpack-eng
gvfs-mtp
gvfs-nfs
gvfs-smb
udiskie

# System - audio/bluetooth/print/network/power daemons, all systemd-service
# or kernel-module backed. Stay system tier.
brightnessctl
pamixer
alsa-utils
wireplumber
pulseaudio-utils
pipewire-pulseaudio
bluez
bluez-tools
plymouth
cups
cups-browsed
cups-filters
cups-pdf
system-config-printer
nss-mdns
avahi
bolt
# power-profiles-daemon NOT listed: this image already ships tuned-ppd,
# which provides the same ppd-service D-Bus interface and hard-conflicts
# with power-profiles-daemon (confirmed: rpm-ostree refused the transaction
# outright - "package tuned-ppd ... conflicts with ppd-service provided by
# power-profiles-daemon"). tuned-ppd already covers what this was for.
firewalld
python3-pip
flatpak
NetworkManager
NetworkManager-tui
nm-connection-editor
# snapper/btrfs-assistant/python3-dnf-plugin-snapper deliberately dropped:
# OSTree has its own atomic rollback (rpm-ostree rollback), a second
# snapshot scheme on top would be redundant and would fight it. See
# install/config/btrfs-snapper.sh, dropped from the secureblue install path
# entirely (install/config/config-secureblue.sh does not call it).
dosfstools
exfatprogs
wireless-regdb
libsecret
gnome-keyring

# Display Manager - SDDM is already the active display manager in the base
# secureblue image; this line is a no-op in practice (rpm -q already true)
# but stays for documentation/idempotency parity with the Fedora list.
sddm

# Fonts - system tier: needed before any session/flatpak sandbox exists.
google-noto-fonts-common
google-noto-sans-fonts
google-noto-sans-cjk-fonts
google-noto-emoji-fonts
google-noto-color-emoji-fonts
jetbrains-mono-fonts
cascadia-mono-nf-fonts
fontawesome4-fonts
fontawesome-fonts-web
terminus-fonts-console

# Input Methods
fcitx5
fcitx5-gtk
fcitx5-qt5
fcitx5-qt6

# First-party tool runtime deps (see omarchy-base.packages.fedora for why -
# same reasoning applies unchanged here)
webkit2gtk4.1
gtk3
gtk-layer-shell
gstreamer1
gstreamer1-plugins-good
ffmpeg-free
alsa-lib
qt6-qtbase
qt6-qtdeclarative
qt6-qtmultimedia
hicolor-icon-theme
libadwaita
libepoxy
fontconfig
gtk4
xdg-desktop-portal
